place transforms.\n\nLet $Y(s)=L{y(t)}.$ Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={{field-3}}.\n$$" 13194139535955 true] [17592186376261 1113 #uuid "31e8084f-ab1a-5236-ac36-9b6a9937b2ed" 13194139535955 true] [17592186376261 1145 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}+5L{y}=L{9{e}^{3t}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative, $L{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0),$ and the standard transform \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{at}}=\\frac{1}{s-a},\\,s>a,\n$$\n\n \nwe substitute the known values.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sY(s)-0+5Y(s) & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-3} \\\\ (s+5)Y(s) & =\\frac{9}{s-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $Y(s),$ we have\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{9}{(s-3)(s+5)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. We write $Y(s)$ in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{(s-3)(s+5)}=\\frac{A}{s-3}+\\frac{B}{s+5}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants $A$ and $B,$ we first clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n9=A(s+5)+B(s-3)\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n9=A(3+5)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,9=8A\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{9}{8}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n9=A(0)+B(-5-3)\\,⟹\\,9=-8B\\,⟹\\,B=-\\frac{9}{8}.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{9}{8(s-3)}-\\frac{9}{8(s+5)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-3}} & ={e}^{3t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+5}} & ={e}^{-5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =\\frac{9}{8}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-3}}-\\frac{9}{8}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+5}} \\\\ y(t) & =\\frac{9}{8}{e}^{3t}-\\frac{9}{8}{e}^{-5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535955 true] [17592186376262 1000 