ults:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nLet $Y(s)=L{y(t)}.$ Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={{field-3}}.\n$$" 13194139535955 true] [17592186376353 1113 #uuid "1873e66d-b721-525d-8ac0-7b13c3d92c58" 13194139535955 true] [17592186376353 1145 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}+2L{y}=L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}-L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative $L{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)$ and the standard trigonometric transforms\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}=\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16},\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-0)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =\\frac{4-s}{{s}^{2}+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ Dividing by $(s+2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{4-s}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+16)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. Since the denominator has a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+16),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\frac{4-s}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+16)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+16}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n4-s=A({s}^{2}+16)+(Bs+C)(s+2)\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n4-(-2)=A((-2)^{2}+16)+0\\,⟹\\,6=20A\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n  \n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,0=\\frac{3}{10}+B\\,⟹\\,B=-\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n \n\n$$\n4=16A+2C\\,⟹\\,4=\\frac{48